# Shared flags of the fixture-cache replay program (`testkit.FixtureCompileCache`), which
# `devkit.FixtureCache` builds into a cache entry of its own:
# it passes `-js <entry>` itself, so the output line cannot live here (haxe rejects a
# second `-js`). `tools/fixture-cache.hxml` is the leaf the compiler oracle typechecks.
#
# No `-cp src` and no `-lib utest`: the replay program is `test/testkit` code alone, so an
# import an ambient source under `test/` adds has to resolve without either.
#
# Why no `-cp src` (a decision, not an omission): `devkit.FixtureCache.shim` names the built
# program by the digest of the one file `test/testkit/FixtureCompileCache.hx` and keeps it in
# the store across runs, so a module imported from `src` could change with no rebuild and a stale
# program would answer every compile. The program is built from the runner's own tree, never a
# mutation slot, so a mutant cannot reach it. Two helpers do re-answer a `src` question: the
# `Reflect.compare` sort (`util.Compare`) and the hxml line reading of `hxmlAllowed`
# (`OracleCache.hxmlArgs`, in the engine's `check.oracle`); the Json sites would need the
# `@:build`-generated `JValueParser`. The sort is one line and the other two sit outside pure
# util/core, so none repays the stale-program risk and the guard a source root would need.
# docs/testing.md, "The fixture cache".
-cp test
-main testkit.FixtureCompileCache
-lib hxnodejs
-D js-es=6
